<h2>Progressive Loading (the Core Principle of Skills)</h2>
<p>The most essential principle behind skills is <strong>progressive loading (progressive disclosure)</strong>. Rather than putting the whole skill into the context, <strong>only the metadata</strong> is loaded up front and the rest is pulled out when it is needed.</p>

<h3>The library book cover analogy</h3>
<p>Only the <strong>metadata (name + description, 3 to 4 lines, about 100 tokens per skill)</strong> of a registered skill goes into the context. It is like a library where the books are lined up on the shelves and you only skim the <strong>covers (title and short description)</strong>, pulling one out to read when you need it. The metadata is so small that even <strong>more than 100</strong> of them barely take up any context. (In contrast to JSON tools, whose full schema occupies the context up front so that the burden grows as their number rises — which is why Claude Code lazy-loads MCP tool schemas too.)</p>

<h3>Three-level loading</h3>
<div class="diagram">
<svg viewBox="0 0 640 252" role="img" aria-label="Three-level progressive loading of a skill: metadata always, the instruction body when invoked, resources and scripts only when needed">
    <rect class="d-box accent" x="20" y="12" width="300" height="60" rx="10"/>
    <text class="d-label" x="170" y="38" text-anchor="middle">① Metadata</text>
    <text class="d-sub" x="170" y="56" text-anchor="middle">name + description · about 100 tokens</text>

    <rect class="d-box" x="20" y="96" width="300" height="60" rx="10"/>
    <text class="d-label" x="170" y="122" text-anchor="middle">② Instruction body</text>
    <text class="d-sub" x="170" y="140" text-anchor="middle">the SKILL.md body</text>

    <rect class="d-box soft" x="20" y="180" width="300" height="60" rx="10"/>
    <text class="d-label" x="170" y="206" text-anchor="middle">③ Resources · scripts</text>
    <text class="d-sub" x="170" y="224" text-anchor="middle">bundled files · templates</text>

    <line class="d-edge" x1="170" y1="72" x2="170" y2="94"/><polygon class="d-arrow" points="165,90 175,90 170,96"/>
    <line class="d-edge" x1="170" y1="156" x2="170" y2="178"/><polygon class="d-arrow" points="165,174 175,174 170,180"/>

    <line class="d-edge dash" x1="320" y1="42" x2="356" y2="42"/>
    <text class="d-accent" x="364" y="46" text-anchor="start">always loaded</text>
    <line class="d-edge dash" x1="320" y1="126" x2="356" y2="126"/>
    <text class="d-accent" x="364" y="130" text-anchor="start">loaded when invoked</text>
    <line class="d-edge dash" x1="320" y1="210" x2="356" y2="210"/>
    <text class="d-accent" x="364" y="214" text-anchor="start">loaded only when needed</text>
</svg>
<div class="d-cap">The higher up, the more it is always loaded; the lower down, the more it is only when needed — the moment of loading gets later at each level.</div>
</div>
<div class="tbl-wrap">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Content</th><th>When it loads</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>1</td><td><strong>Metadata</strong> (the name and description in SKILL.md)</td><td><strong>Always</strong> loaded (about 100 tokens per skill)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2</td><td><strong>Instructions</strong> (the SKILL.md body, usage in markdown)</td><td>When that skill <strong>is invoked</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td>3</td><td><strong>Resources · scripts · templates</strong> (bundled files)</td><td><strong>Only when needed</strong> (scripts are only executed, never loaded)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p>Bundled files are not mandatory. If words (markdown) alone are enough you can do without files, and if you need an executable script or a template you include one. The more files there are, the more <strong>deterministic the result</strong> you get. For reference, the official guide recommends keeping the <code>SKILL.md</code> body <strong>under 5K tokens</strong> and splitting long content out into bundled files.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">The manual analogy (flat-pack products · the Matrix helicopter)</span>
    When you buy a flat-pack product online you skip the manual and assemble it from the photo of the finished item, and only <strong>the moment a screw is left out</strong> do you pull the manual out. A skill's instructions are likewise <strong>pulled out exactly when it fires</strong> and left alone the rest of the time. It feels like the scene in the film The Matrix where they <strong>load a manual in an instant</strong> in front of a helicopter and fly it.
</div>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">A tip for checking</span>
    Typing <code>/context</code> in Claude Code shows what percentage the system prompt, custom tools, skills, and so on each take up. You can confirm that even a large number of installed skills uses barely any tokens.
</div>
